WEBVTT

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:05.240
Bubblegum Crisis is a series with historic significance to both the Japanese and Western anime fandom.

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:10.320
For Japan, it was one of the major titles released during the OVA boom of the mid-1980s.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:16.460
Overseas, it was one of the earliest titles brought over and given an English dub that accurately reflected the Japanese script.

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:23.240
And unlike other titles available at that time, the barrier of entry was way lower for viewers less familiar with anime and Japanese culture,

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:26.820
as the series was heavily influenced by Western films and music videos.

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:29.960
The majority of the cast even have Western names, which

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.160
made it way more easier to follow for newcomers.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:35.800
What's most interesting about Bubblegum Crisis's initial success, though,

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:40.370
is that many of its themes and concepts actually spawned from previous failed anime

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:43.070
produced by its creator, Toshimichi Suzuki.

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:46.190
I say initial success because throughout its production history,

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.780
the series was plagued with budget and legal issues,

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:51.420
eventually leading to the series' premature cancellation.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:54.460
The legal issues wouldn't end with the series' cancellation, though,

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:57.340
as the fallout would send Suzuki on a downward spiral,

00:00:57.340 --> 00:00:59.980
eventually involving the Yakuza and his own personality.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.900
exile, with his ultimate fate unknown to the public for almost 20 years.

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:15.740
Artmik was a design studio founded in 1978 by former Tatsunoko producer Toshimichi Suzuki.

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:20.590
Artmik wasn't a traditional animation studio though, as they focused on story and design

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:24.910
work outsourcing the actual animation to other companies, usually AIC.

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:29.180
This was similar to Studio Nui, who at the time were most known for their work on Space

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:29.980
Battleship Yamato.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.520
Artmec's first project was called Techno Police 21C and was about a cop in the future assigned to a special task force

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:39.500
that would use robot assistants to give them an edge on the criminals.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:43.900
The staff consisted of industry veterans due to Suzuki's connections from Tatsunoko,

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:48.300
including names like Yoshitaka Amano and future Ghibli composer Joe Hisaishi.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:51.740
The theme was performed by Mokoto Fujiwara, best known for

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:59.970
And for the mech design, Suzuki would get Studio Nue's own Shoji Kawamori and Kazutaka Miyatake, also best known for

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.460
known for. Despite the all-star team behind the scenes, the project fell through, with only

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:11.100
scattered bits of animation done for the first few episodes. Suzuki decided that the best he could do

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:15.700
to try to recoup his loss was by releasing the animation that was already completed as a film.

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:20.980
The film was released by Toho Eizo and dubbed in English in Hong Kong, using the same voice actors

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:26.940
they would use to dub Godzilla movies. You are in no position to handle this tank, so I've called in

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:29.800
the fighters. They'll be here within the hour. What's that?

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:33.420
Have you gone mad? We can't do that. We can't have a war here.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:37.260
Techno Police flopped in theaters and received predominantly negative reception,

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:43.220
but the concept remained in Suzuki's mind, as one that he felt he could revisit once he had the proper resources.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:47.360
Not long after the failure of Techno Police in late 1982,

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:54.100
art mix Shinji Aramaki and Hideki Kagenuma began pitching a concept they'd developed that was similar to Techno Police.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:59.960
The series was titled A.D. Patrol, and it was set in a futuristic city where cops chased criminals using a...

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.100
advanced motorcycles that could transform

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:03.340
into their robot partners.

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:05.900
At the time, the anime Macross was in the middle

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:07.220
of its initial TV run,

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:09.320
and the series was pulling in great ratings.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:12.120
As a result, Macross's sponsor Takutoku

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:14.680
told Aramaki and Kakenuma that they should change

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:16.380
their premise, making it about a war

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:17.580
between humans and aliens.

00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:20.430
This was because Takutoku was looking for an anime

00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:22.290
to take over Macross's time slot

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:24.390
after the series completed, and for a moment,

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:26.350
it seemed like Aramaki and Kakenuma

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:27.630
had what they were looking for.

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:29.850
They adjusted their premise to be about humans

00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:29.970
Thank you.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.180
having to reclaim Earth from alien invaders.

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:35.540
Kakenuma said his initial idea was for a sci-fi version

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:37.980
of the 1962 film The Longest Day,

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:39.820
with soldiers dropping onto the planet

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:42.900
and charging the alien base using transforming motorcycles,

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:45.340
similar to the storming of the beaches of Normandy.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:47.640
Due to the popularity of the character Minmei,

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:50.100
they were also told that the anime needed to have a singer,

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:51.720
leading to the creation of a character

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:53.300
known at the time as Nora.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:55.200
Since Minmei embodied the wholesome

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:57.820
girl-next-door archetype seen in most idols of the time,

00:03:57.820 --> 00:03:59.980
It was decided that Nora would be based off

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.620
idol Akina Nakamori. Nakamori was relatively new to the idol scene, but made waves due to her

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:11.040
rebellious attitude, provocative lyrics, and distinct powerhouse voice. She was also considered

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:16.360
the rival to Seiko Matsuda, who was the idol that originally inspired Minmay. Noro was a rebel,

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:20.860
a rock singer, and a former biker. However, everything changed when scenario writer

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:26.480
Sugihiro Tomida saw Yoshitaka Amano's design for the character Yellow Belmont. When Tomida saw the

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:29.970
He said that's our singer originally yellow Belmont was a

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.880
former soldier, but at Tomita's request was given dual identities, one of a man who used to be a

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:39.540
soldier, and the other of a female singer, and the character would alternate between a male and female

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:42.140
voice actor, depending on which identity was assumed.

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:54.810
The character Nora was re-versioned into the character Hukai at Rose, and though she's still a loner and a former

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:59.750
member of a biker gang, her role as the musician was revoked. Despite this, her finalized character

00:04:59.750 --> 00:04:59.970
sheet.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.920
actually still lists her hobbies as musical performance, but this is simply an oversight.

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:10.540
According to Aramaki, Takatoku said the motorcycle idea was too weak to be its own show, suggesting

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:14.900
more changes to make the show similar to Macross, the biggest being the assistance of a fighter

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:18.360
jet that could transform into a mech, leading to the creation of the Legios.

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:23.460
The series would be titled Genesis Climber Mospeeda, Mospeeda being the name of the transforming

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:24.040
motorcycle.

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:28.160
However, the Legios would be pushed to the forefront at the behest of the advertisers,

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:29.960
to the point that by only a few

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:35.280
episodes into the show, the titular mech had become obsolete. It's unclear why Taketoku passed

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:40.180
on Mosbita, choosing Orgus as the successor of Macross, but when asked, Shinji Aramaki said it

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:45.400
was due to political reasons. Mosbita would have fallen into the same pitfall as Techno Police if

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:49.780
not for plastic model company Gaken, who heard of the proposal and arranged a meeting where they

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:54.060
agreed to sponsor the show. Though not to the degree of Techno Police, Mosbita flopped hard,

00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:58.740
but Artbrick would soon discover a new avenue for their talent, an avenue that they themselves would

00:05:58.740 --> 00:05:59.300
Pioneer.

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:10.480
History repeated itself in the worst way during the production of Omega City 2-3, eventually renamed Megazone 2-3.

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:16.900
The series suffered from budgeting issues after the plastic model company lined up to produce the kits for the series backed out of their arrangement.

00:06:17.140 --> 00:06:21.460
As a result, the animation done for the series was combined to create a standalone anime film.

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:28.420
The film had a short theatrical run before its release on VHS, and despite plot holes and an incomplete ending, audiences loved it.

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:29.860
and Megazone is often credited

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:33.940
as opening the floodgates for the OVA boom of the 80s and it was during this

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:38.340
anime boom that Artmec would shine. In December of 1985 at the wrap party for

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:43.240
Fightixer 1, Suzuki met Junji Fujita, the founder of Yomex. Yomex was a

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:46.600
subsidiary of Toshiba EMI who were looking to invest in the growing

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:50.240
straight-to-video anime market. Fujita asked for a pitch and Suzuki gave him

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:53.400
the skeleton of his Techno Police remake. After some brainstorming with

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:57.660
Fujita the concept began to shift. In 1984 the film Streets of Fire flopped in

00:06:57.660 --> 00:06:59.820
in the US, but in Japan it was a hit.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:01.280
becoming a cultural touchstone.

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:03.580
Suzuki initially considered changing the story

00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:05.660
to a sci-fi retelling of Streets of Fire,

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:07.500
which would allow them to recycle their concept

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:08.680
for the female rock singer,

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:10.760
as well as utilize Yomex's connections

00:07:10.760 --> 00:07:11.760
in the record industry.

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:15.360
However, two other films released between 1982 and 1985

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:17.720
would also influence the makeup of this project.

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:21.300
Those would be 1982's Blade Runner and 1984's Terminator.

00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:24.160
Suzuki's original concept focused on the AD police,

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:26.900
the special police force in the bubblegum crisis universe

00:07:26.900 --> 00:07:28.660
who investigate boomer crimes.

00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:29.860
Boomers being the name.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:34.820
advanced androids. Though originally influenced by Techno Police and the premise for AD Patrol,

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:40.980
the AD Police in the finished version appear more like Blade Runners. The AD Police wouldn't hold the spotlight for long, as

00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:47.760
Suzuki decided he wanted the heroes to be mercenaries. Soldiers for hire who'd wear hard suits to fight against boomers when political reasons

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:54.180
prevented the AD Police from doing so. This aspect was inspired by the Jidai Geki drama Hisatsu Shio-Kin-Yen,

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:59.980
which was about merchants in the Edo period who'd moonlight as assassins. Hence why the Night Saber's leader Celia Starr

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:06.340
Stingray is shown operating a lingerie store in the first episode. For the designs of the hard suits, Suzuki showed character designer

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:09.740
Kenichi Sonoda the music video for the song Go! by the band Asia.

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:13.620
Sonoda noted that the woman in the music video was wearing a form-fitting suit,

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:19.400
but thought it would look more futuristic if she was wearing full body armor. When it came to developing the individual characters,

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:27.750
Suzuki wanted an all-female cast due to the profitable returns seen on the release of Ixer-1 and Golf Force, which were both female-driven OVAs.

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:29.980
Fujita's involvement led to Suzuki having

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:31.920
having access to professional music talent,

00:08:32.100 --> 00:08:34.300
and so the main character would be a female singer,

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:37.440
with Suzuki almost completely recycling the Nora character.

00:08:37.830 --> 00:08:39.450
Pris, named for the Blade Runner character,

00:08:39.730 --> 00:08:41.350
was a former biker and rock singer

00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:43.110
who preferred to do things her own way.

00:08:43.330 --> 00:08:45.350
She even had a similar backstory to Hukai.

00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:47.650
Hukai left her biker gang after her boyfriend

00:08:47.650 --> 00:08:49.650
abandoned her during a fight with a rival gang,

00:08:49.910 --> 00:08:51.730
whereas Pris was a biker whose boyfriend

00:08:51.730 --> 00:08:52.890
was killed by a boomer,

00:08:53.090 --> 00:08:54.810
a crime which was never brought to justice

00:08:54.810 --> 00:08:56.590
due to the corruption caused by Genom.

00:08:56.810 --> 00:08:58.850
Pris' voice was provided by J-Rock singer,

00:08:58.850 --> 00:08:59.810
Kinuko Omori.

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:04.780
who had no acting experience and stood out for her deep voice among the other female voice actors.

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:10.480
The first installment of Bubblegum Crisis, Tinsel City, dropped on February 25th, 1987.

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:12.270
The video was a hit.

00:09:12.620 --> 00:09:18.120
In response, two more OVAs continuing the story were released in September and December of that year,

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:20.980
albeit with shorter run times due to budget constraints.

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:28.560
At the time of Bubblegum Crisis's release, an OVA on VHS cost roughly 10,000 yen, or about 91 US dollars.

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:29.920
Because Bubblegum Crisis

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:35.440
was a series requiring potential buyers to commit to buying multiple episodes the price was reduced

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:41.850
to 68 000 yen or about 60 usd this meant the series was on a tight budget though in an an

00:09:41.850 --> 00:09:46.650
america interview suzuki said it was a necessary sacrifice and as a result the team had to choose

00:09:46.650 --> 00:09:51.150
what information would make it into animation and what would have to be explained in supplemental

00:09:51.150 --> 00:09:56.390
material like novels and drama cds as a result character backgrounds are mostly absent from the

00:09:56.390 --> 00:09:59.970
series, with one of the few exceptions being Celia Stingray, who...

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:04.480
as a vague flashback suggesting Genom's Vice President Brian Mason murdered her father.

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:09.380
Linna and Pris' backstories are nowhere to be seen in the series, though we see flashes of them

00:10:09.380 --> 00:10:14.220
in the music video for Asuei Touchdown, which was included at the end of the third videotape.

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:22.250
Following the release of the third episode, a major issue arose that threatened the longevity

00:10:22.250 --> 00:10:27.250
of the series. In 1988, Omori signed a new recording contract with Sony Records,

00:10:27.250 --> 00:10:29.970
and a stipulation of that contract prevented her from

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:31.900
from singing on the Bubblegum Crisis soundtrack.

00:10:32.200 --> 00:10:33.780
This caused tension behind the scenes,

00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:35.860
as the entire point of hiring Omori

00:10:35.860 --> 00:10:37.680
was so Pris could sing her own songs.

00:10:37.880 --> 00:10:40.340
And so it was decided that Pris would be killed,

00:10:40.600 --> 00:10:43.200
only to be replaced with a new character named Vision.

00:10:43.420 --> 00:10:44.580
Vision was also a singer,

00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:46.680
and would be the sister of Linna's friend Irene,

00:10:47.080 --> 00:10:48.300
who was killed in episode two.

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:50.800
However, when fans heard of the plan to kill Pris,

00:10:50.900 --> 00:10:51.640
there was an outrage,

00:10:51.860 --> 00:10:53.660
and many said they would abandon the series

00:10:53.660 --> 00:10:55.020
if Omori left the project.

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:56.680
Omori was kept on the project,

00:10:56.820 --> 00:10:58.560
and Yomex continued to release songs,

00:10:58.560 --> 00:10:59.980
only they were now credited

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:04.680
it as Pris and the Night Sabers, and contained ensemble vocals to avoid legal issues.

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:09.260
The consistency of its music was important because Bubblegum Crisis was unparalleled

00:11:09.260 --> 00:11:12.040
in terms of both its quantity and quality of music.

00:11:12.460 --> 00:11:17.260
Yomex made sure each OVA contained an album's worth of songs, many of which contained vocal

00:11:17.260 --> 00:11:20.780
tracks which made them more desirable than your average anime OST.

00:11:21.020 --> 00:11:24.920
It's worth mentioning though that many of these songs have similarities to pre-existing

00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:29.980
pop songs, with some being clearly inspired and others being outright plagiarized.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:30.180
list.

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:04.390
No, John, you're dead on me.

00:12:44.840 --> 00:12:47.500
Episode three, the final episode released in 1987,

00:12:47.980 --> 00:12:50.020
saw the Night Sabers defeat Brian Mason,

00:12:50.220 --> 00:12:51.840
but due to the popularity of the series,

00:12:51.980 --> 00:12:52.960
they were able to continue,

00:12:53.260 --> 00:12:56.400
with Suzuki stating he planned for 13 episodes overall.

00:12:56.780 --> 00:12:57.860
Episodes four through six

00:12:57.860 --> 00:12:59.800
would see the rise of a new villain, Largo.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:03.680
a boomer seeking revenge on both the Nightsabers and the Ganom Corporation,

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:05.800
inspired by Blade Runner's Roy Batty.

00:13:06.020 --> 00:13:09.820
Largo would be defeated in Episode 6, which released in August of 1989.

00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:13.460
The next episode was Double Vision, released in March of 1990,

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:16.760
and recycled the planned Vision story, minus the death of Pris.

00:13:17.020 --> 00:13:22.040
But as the 90s began, Japan's bubble economy was ending, and anime sales across the country were dropping.

00:13:22.240 --> 00:13:26.760
Despite having six more episodes planned, the eighth episode of Bubblegum Crisis would be its last,

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:30.000
and it seemed like ArtMik and AIC weren't ready to come to terms with it.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:35.280
Since the episode is essentially a filler episode filled with slice-of-life gags and no sense of finality.

00:13:35.480 --> 00:13:43.000
Following episode 8, Yomex and Art Mc parted ways. However, Art Mc continued the Bubblegum Crisis story with animation company AIC, and in May of

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:49.700
1991, they released the first episode of Bubblegum Crash, a new OVA series intended to complete the story of the original Bubblegum

00:13:49.700 --> 00:13:57.180
Crisis. Crash was a direct sequel to Crisis, though several plot details are omitted, possibly due to rights issues since Yomex wasn't involved anymore.

00:13:57.420 --> 00:13:59.080
The production of Crash is very sketchy

00:13:59.290 --> 00:13:59.930
as characters

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:04.160
designer Kenichi Tsunoda said he had left Artmik prior to production of the first episode.

00:14:04.340 --> 00:14:08.300
The voice cast would return with the exception of Amori, who following the end of Crisis wanted

00:14:08.300 --> 00:14:12.780
to focus more on her music career, as her band Silk was way more popular now than when

00:14:12.780 --> 00:14:14.280
she was originally cast as Pris.

00:14:14.620 --> 00:14:21.080
Without Yomex, the music suffered and Crash would ultimately have a generic soundtrack lacking any of the excitement of the Crysis soundtrack.

00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:23.960
Even the stolen music in Crash isn't as good.

00:14:37.510 --> 00:14:38.790
Crash would meet a similar demise,

00:14:38.930 --> 00:14:41.170
only this time it was due to direct legal issues

00:14:41.170 --> 00:14:43.210
from Yomex, who claimed partial ownership

00:14:43.210 --> 00:14:44.750
over the Bubblegum Crisis IP.

00:14:45.070 --> 00:14:46.690
Yomex came at Artmik with a lawsuit

00:14:46.690 --> 00:14:48.830
that put the series in limbo until 1997.

00:14:49.370 --> 00:14:51.090
It's believed that Crash was supposed to run

00:14:51.090 --> 00:14:53.990
for five or six episodes, but only ran for three.

00:14:54.250 --> 00:14:56.250
By then, Artmik had defaulted on their debt

00:14:56.250 --> 00:14:57.370
and declared bankruptcy.

00:14:57.670 --> 00:14:59.010
All their assets were liquidated,

00:14:59.030 --> 00:14:59.970
and many of the properties

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:02.460
including Bubblegum Crisis, were purchased by AIC.

00:15:02.720 --> 00:15:05.940
A year later in 1998, the debt they had acquired from ART-MIC

00:15:05.940 --> 00:15:08.920
led to Yomex being absorbed back into Toshiba EMI.

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:12.680
Toshimichi Suzuki had disappeared along with many of ART-MIC's assets,

00:15:12.900 --> 00:15:15.160
including original art pieces by Kenichi Tsunoda.

00:15:15.420 --> 00:15:17.640
After legally acquiring the rights to Bubblegum Crisis

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:19.680
along with several other ART-MIC properties,

00:15:20.020 --> 00:15:23.020
AIC entered production on a Bubblegum Crisis TV series

00:15:23.020 --> 00:15:24.880
that retold the events of the OVA.

00:15:25.140 --> 00:15:27.480
It was around this time that Suzuki re-emerged,

00:15:29.290 --> 00:15:29.810
and alongside

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:30.740
several Yakuza.

00:15:31.020 --> 00:15:34.760
Suzuki claimed AIC owed him royalties over Bubblegum Crisis 2040.

00:15:34.960 --> 00:15:38.500
AIC said they didn't owe him anything, and when he tried to intimidate them with Yakuza,

00:15:38.760 --> 00:15:41.260
AIC just called the cops and had the Yakuza arrested.

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:46.140
Suzuki escaped this altercation but went into hiding disappearing from the public eye for 20 years

00:15:46.810 --> 00:15:51.870
Speculation ranged from him fleeing the country to him being killed by other yakuza due to his real estate debts

00:15:51.870 --> 00:15:56.670
It was only in September of 2020 that we'd get an update from Suzuki's son himself

00:15:56.670 --> 00:15:59.950
Suzuki's son had discovered the speculation over his father's wear

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:03.960
and created a Twitter account for the sole purpose of updating the world on his father.

00:16:04.160 --> 00:16:09.000
Following the altercation with AIC, Suzuki went into hiding, living in complete seclusion out of shame.

00:16:09.220 --> 00:16:12.200
Both because of his extensive debt, as well as his criminal actions.

00:16:12.420 --> 00:16:17.180
He survived by working as a painter. His son even found a photo of a painting on Suzuki's cell phone,

00:16:17.340 --> 00:16:19.460
which he believed his father had done for a client.

00:16:19.660 --> 00:16:25.540
These updates came at the bitter expense of learning that Suzuki had died earlier that year, in late January of 2020.

00:16:28.860 --> 00:16:29.920
In 2018 character

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:33.860
Character designer Kenichi Sonoda launched a Kickstarter to create a new OVA based on

00:16:33.860 --> 00:16:35.280
his manga Gunsmith Cats.

00:16:35.540 --> 00:16:39.260
The Kickstarter was successful, and when asked if he'd ever considered working on a Bubblegum

00:16:39.260 --> 00:16:41.020
Crisis revival, Sonoda stated,

00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:52.620
For those people watching for the first time, I would love to recommend parts 1 through 8,

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:54.580
but you don't need to watch Bubblegum Crash.

00:17:01.720 --> 00:17:03.480
Kim Hee-Chi

00:17:07.700 --> 00:17:09.550
So-Re-So-Re-Nya
